\vspace{-2mm}
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-1 We identify symbolic confirmation bias: self-extracted constraints inherit the generating model's biases ($\text{BR} = 4.97\times$, 83.3\%), explaining why twenty-seven remediation strategies fail. Oracle controls ($+29.90$/$+14.22$\,pp) confirm the mechanism is sound; the bottleneck is extraction quality, not the formal channel. Three diagnostics predict failure ($C_0 < 5\%$, SC $\leq 40\%$, process similarity). Cross-architecture NLI escapes the bias when the verifier exceeds the generator ($p = 0.003$); DRC recovers 83--90\% of this gain at 35--39\% cost.

\section{Cross-Architecture Proof-of-Concept}
\label{sec:solutions}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\paragraph{Method.}
\looseness=-1 We test with DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}, classifying (premise, conclusion) pairs as entailment, contradiction, or neutral. This proof-of-concept is limited to propositional-logic benchmarks whose premise-conclusion structure naturally suits NLI; generalization to non-logical domains remains untested. In combo mode, each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times alongside SC's $K$ votes. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) and ProofWriter depth-5 ($n = 600$).

\paragraph{Main result.}
\looseness=-1 DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO (below SC's 55.88\%) and 55.00\% on ProofWriter (above SC's 39.33\%). On FOLIO ($n = 204$), combo $w{=}3$ yields $+2.94$\,pp (n.s.). On ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), combo $w{=}3$ achieves significant gains for Mistral-7B ($+6.67$\,pp, $p < 0.001$) and LLaMA-8B ($+4.17$\,pp, $p = 0.004$); stronger generators show no improvement---while same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter ($-2.83$\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), illustrating that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). After BH correction ($m{=}12$), Mistral-7B ($w{=}3$, $w{=}5$) and LLaMA-8B ($w{=}1$, $w{=}3$) remain significant.

\paragraph{Why it works.}
\looseness=-1 Our preliminary results suggest three conditions jointly enable the escape---structural independence, positive capability gap, and task-verifier alignment---expanded in \S\ref{sec:discussion}. NLI classification operates on (premise, conclusion) pairs, a different task from constraint extraction; the success may partly reflect task-format alignment. Per-label analysis confirms NLI and SC excel on complementary labels (Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}); all three NLI architectures produce gains at $w \geq 3$.

\paragraph{Capability gap hypothesis.}
\looseness=-1 Four generators spanning a 46-point SC range on ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), all paired with DeBERTa-large-mnli (55.00\% standalone; Table~\ref{tab:cross_generator_nli}): Mistral-7B (SC 39.33\%, below the verifier) gains $+6.67$\,pp ($p < 0.001$) and LLaMA-8B (SC 70.00\%, above the verifier) gains $+4.17$\,pp ($p = 0.004$); generators above SC $\approx 74\%$ show no improvement. Per-label analysis confirms NLI and SC excel on complementary labels (Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}), with complementarity diminishing as the generator improves across all labels. The monotonic relationship holds across three NLI architectures and six verifier-generator pairs (Figure~\ref{fig:capability-gap}; Appendix~\ref{app:capability-gap}). A $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix confirms BR $> 1$ in all eight cells ($1.94$--$2.70$; Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}).

